  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 333 PM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nFriday and Saturday...\n\nBroad upper-level troughing over the Midwest and Great Lakes will \nsettle in by the end of the week and continue into the weekend, \nwhile ridging over the western Atlantic and southeast CONUS will \nsettle in. A surface boundary will linger off to our north over \nnorthern Indiana, Illinois, and Ohio, keeping our region in an area \nof higher moisture (LREF PW > 1.7\") and in the path of multiple \nshortwaves moving through. Some of the higher chances for showers \nand isolated-to-scattered thunderstorms will take place late Friday \nthrough midday Saturday (45-65%), with the main concerns again being \nheavier downpours, frequent lightning, and gusty winds. Deep layer \nshear will still be relatively weak (< 20 kt), so any concerns for \nstrong to severe storms is still very minimal. Southerly flow will \nalso make conditions hot and muggy, with highs in the upper 80s/low \n90s and heat indices in the mid-to-upper 90s.\n\nSunday through Middle Next Week...\n\nThe upper trough over the Midwest/Great Lakes becomes less amplified \nas ridging from the Atlantic/SE CONUS high tries to build westward. \nThe mid/upper-level flow over our region becomes more zonal in \nnature, with PWs also decreasing some (most recent LREF outputs 1.4-\n1.6\" PW). The jet stream will be further north, keeping more of the \ntraversing shortwaves off to the north and reducing our chances for \nprecipitation into the early parts of next week. Southerly surface \nflow will keep temperatures in the upper 80s/low 90s Sunday through \nTuesday before the frontal boundary begins to move southward. The \nshifting of the front will be in part due to the amplification of \nthe western CONUS ridge, as well as a deepening of an upper level \ntrough over the Hudson Bay. LREF cluster guidance does show \nvariability in the timing and progression of these features, with \nthe mean output bringing the front through Tuesday night into \nWednesday. Regardless of timing, this will bring back higher chances \nfor showers and storms (40-60% chance) ahead of the front as it \nmoves through the region.",
